course: dictionary
lesson: 14283
title: Nosiness and privacy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Nosiness means being too interested in other people's private business. Private business is things that belong to someone else, like their diary, their post, or what they are whispering about. «Ben listened at the bedroom door and asked what everyone was whispering about.» Ben is showing nosiness, because the whispers were not his business.
explain: Being curious is not the same as being nosy. Asking a friend how her holiday was is friendly. Reading her diary when she has left the room is nosiness, because it takes something private that she did not share with you. A good check is to ask yourself: would the other person want me to know this?
explain: The word is a noun, so you can say «Her nosiness annoyed her brother» or «The nosiness of the neighbour was famous on the street.» Nosiness is an uncountable noun, so do not add an s to it. Nosy is the describing word: «a nosy neighbour».
explain: Nosiness is also a safety word online. «Never tell a stranger in a game where you live.» A stranger who keeps asking for your address, school or photos is showing nosiness about private things. Keep private details private, and tell a trusted adult if someone keeps asking.
fact: Nosiness means being too interested in other people's private business.
fact: Nosiness is an uncountable noun, so we say «a lot of nosiness», not «nosinesses».
fact: A nosy person is someone who shows nosiness. Nosy is the describing word.
fact: Synonyms for nosiness include prying, snooping and meddling, but each has its own shade of meaning.
fact: In stories, a nosy neighbour's nosiness may uncover a mystery, like spotting a strange visitor at midnight.
exercise: «Her nosiness made her listen at the door.» What is nosiness? | Being very tired after a long day | Being kind to new neighbours | Being good at maths | *Being too interested in other people's private business
exercise: Mum says, «Stop your nosiness, Sam, that letter is for Dad.» What does Mum mean? | *You are being too interested in something that is not your business | You are being a good helper | You have lost the letter | You must write a letter too
exercise: Which example shows nosiness? | *Reading your friend's diary when she has left the room | Asking the teacher about homework | Sharing your sandwiches at lunch | Helping your friend carry her bags
test: Which example of nosiness online is most risky? | *Asking a stranger in a chat for your friend's home address | Saying thank you to a teacher | Sharing your favourite game with a friend | Writing a story about dragons
test: Which word is closest in meaning to nosiness? | *Prying | Silence | Kindness | Laughter
exam: Which sentence shows nosiness? | Ben gave his seat to an older lady on the bus | Ben shared his football cards with his brother | *Ben listened at the bedroom door and asked what everyone was whispering about | Ben said sorry for being late
